Access-based consumption models (rental, sharing, leasing) represent fundamental shifts from ownership to use-rights, potentially unlocking circular benefits like extended utilization and optimized product lifespans. However, consumer adoption depends on overcoming psychological and practical barriers associated with relinquishing ownership. Users must perceive access-based models as convenient, trustworthy, and comparable in value to ownership. Design for access acceptance includes product durability to withstand shared use, insurance and liability clarity, digital convenience, and ensuring psychological ownership of temporary use experiences.